#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:9
msgid "Learning about nodes - tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:11
msgid ""
"Nodes are the fundamental building blocks of a robotic system, where each "
"node is responsible for a single task. This article walks you through how to "
"run nodes with the ``ros2`` command-line tools and inspect how they are "
"shown on the ROS graph. A hands-on exercise gives you practice listing "
"active nodes and examining their connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:15
msgid ""
"**Area: Nodes, Framework | Content-type: tutorial | Experience: beginner**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:19
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:22
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:24
msgid ""
"Each node in ROS serves a single, modular purpose in a robotics system. For "
"more information, see :doc:`About nodes <../../../About-Nodes>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:27
msgid ""
"Nodes communicate with other nodes through :doc:`topics, services, actions, "
"and parameters <../../../Interfaces-Topics-Services-Actions>`. A single "
"executable can contain one or more nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:30
msgid ""
"For more information, see :doc:`How ROS works <../../../How-ROS-Works>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:33
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:35
msgid ""
":doc:`Using turtlesim, ros2, and rqt <../../../../Get-Started/Introducing-"
"Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim>` shows you how to install the ``turtlesim`` "
"package used here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:38
msgid "Make sure to source ROS in every new terminal you open."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:40
msgid ""
"For more information, see :doc:`Configuring environment <../../../../Get-"
"Started/Configuring-ROS2-Environment>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:43
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:46
msgid "1 Launch an executable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:48
msgid ""
"Nodes run inside executables. To add a node to the ROS graph, launch an "
"executable from a package with the ``ros2 run`` command, in the following "
"format:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:51
msgid "$ ros2 run <package_name> <executable_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:55
msgid ""
"To run the ``turtlesim_node`` executable from the ``turtlesim`` package, "
"open a new terminal and enter the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:57
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:61
msgid ""
"The Turtlesim window opens, as shown in :doc:`Using turtlesim, ros2, and rqt "
"<../../../../Get-Started/Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:63
msgid ""
"In this example, the package name is ``turtlesim`` and the executable name "
"is ``turtlesim_node``. The executable name is not always the same as the "
"node name on the ROS graph. You can find node names by using ``ros2 node "
"list``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:68
msgid "2 List all running nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:70
msgid ""
"``ros2 node list`` shows you the names of all running nodes. This is "
"especially useful when you want to interact with a node, or when your system "
"runs many nodes and you need to keep track of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:73
msgid ""
"While Turtlesim is still running in the other terminal, open a new terminal "
"and enter the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:75
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:95
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:125
msgid "$ ros2 node list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:79
msgid "The terminal returns the node name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:81
msgid "/turtlesim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:85
msgid ""
"To see how the list changes when another node starts, in another new "
"terminal, start a teleoperation node:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:87
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtle_teleop_key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:91
msgid ""
"Here, we are referring to the ``turtlesim`` package again, but this time we "
"target the executable named ``turtle_teleop_key``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:93
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:123
msgid ""
"Return to the terminal where you ran ``ros2 node list`` and run it again:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:99
msgid "You should see the names of two running nodes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:101
msgid ""
"/turtlesim\n"
"/teleop_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:107
msgid "2.1 Remap a node name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:109
msgid ""
"`Remapping <https://design.ros2.org/articles/ros_command_line_arguments."
"html#name-remapping-rules>`__ allows you to reassign default node "
"properties, such as node name, topic names, or service names, to custom "
"values. In :doc:`Using turtlesim, ros2, and rqt <../../../../Get-Started/"
"Introducing-Turtlesim/Introducing-Turtlesim>`, you use remapping on "
"``turtle_teleop_key`` to change the ``cmd_vel`` topic and target **turtle2**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:112
msgid ""
"You can also remap the node name when you start a new node. In a new "
"terminal, run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:115
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node --ros-args --remap __node:=my_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:119
msgid ""
"This starts a second Turtlesim node named ``my_turtle``. It does not rename "
"the ``/turtlesim`` node that is already running. Another Turtlesim window "
"opens for the new node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:129
msgid "You will see three node names:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:131
msgid ""
"/my_turtle\n"
"/turtlesim\n"
"/teleop_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:138
msgid "3 Access more information about nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:140
msgid ""
"Now that you know the names of your nodes, you can see how a node connects "
"to the rest of the system. The ``ros2 node info`` command shows a node's "
"publishers, subscribers, services, and actions. These are the ROS graph "
"connections that interact with that node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:144
msgid "$ ros2 node info <node_name>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:148
msgid "To examine your latest node, ``my_turtle``, run the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:150
msgid "$ ros2 node info /my_turtle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:154
msgid "You should see output similar to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:156
msgid ""
"/my_turtle\n"
"  Subscribers:\n"
"    /parameter_events: rcl_interfaces/msg/ParameterEvent\n"
"    /turtle1/cmd_vel: geometry_msgs/msg/Twist\n"
"  Publishers:\n"
"    /parameter_events: rcl_interfaces/msg/ParameterEvent\n"
"    /rosout: rcl_interfaces/msg/Log\n"
"    /turtle1/color_sensor: turtlesim_msgs/msg/Color\n"
"    /turtle1/pose: turtlesim_msgs/msg/Pose\n"
"  Service Servers:\n"
"    /clear: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"    /kill: turtlesim_msgs/srv/Kill\n"
"    /my_turtle/describe_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/DescribeParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/get_parameter_types: rcl_interfaces/srv/GetParameterTypes\n"
"    /my_turtle/get_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/GetParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/list_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/ListParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/set_parameters: rcl_interfaces/srv/SetParameters\n"
"    /my_turtle/set_parameters_atomically: rcl_interfaces/srv/"
"SetParametersAtomically\n"
"    /reset: std_srvs/srv/Empty\n"
"    /spawn: turtlesim_msgs/srv/Spawn\n"
"    /turtle1/set_pen: turtlesim_msgs/srv/SetPen\n"
"    /turtle1/teleport_absolute: turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportAbsolute\n"
"    /turtle1/teleport_relative: turtlesim_msgs/srv/TeleportRelative\n"
"  Service Clients:\n"
"\n"
"  Action Servers:\n"
"    /turtle1/rotate_absolute: turtlesim_msgs/action/RotateAbsolute\n"
"  Action Clients:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.rst:187
msgid ""
"Now try running the same command on the ``/teleop_turtle`` node, and see how "
"its connections differ from ``my_turtle``."
msgstr ""

